#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192389 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d363c839-34b6-55c7-85d8-f2013e08cc62" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "2393d607-0f73-59f3-9a5f-ebe45050dda9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10106" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim N(\\theta, 1),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown mean. Find the likelihood function of the sample $0.4,\\, -0.3,\\, -2.2,\\, -0.4.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = 1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{x-\\theta}{\\sigma} \\right)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i$ and $\\displaystyle C = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i^2.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that $n=4,$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.4+(-0.3)+(-2.2)+(-0.4) \\\\  & =-2.5 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.4)^{2}+(-0.3)^{2}+(-2.2)^{2}+(-0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =5.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-4/2}\\,{e}^{-(5.25-2(-2.5)\\theta +4{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{-2.625\\,-\\,2.5\\theta \\,-\\,2{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6068f85b-0ea6-5161-a8d1-d4eb2504852b" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Exponential Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "2ff20920-30c5-5027-b461-31b13d2031e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-11121" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_6$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim \\text{Exp}(\\theta)$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta.$ Find the likelihood function of the sample $1.5, \\, 2.0, \\, 1.1, \\, 0.4, \\, 3.3, \\, 2.7.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\,\\theta) = \\theta e^{-\\theta x}, \\qquad x\\geq 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{1}})\\cdot (\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{2}})⋯(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta ({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+⋯+{x}_{n})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i.$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1.5+2.0+1.1+0.4+3.3+2.7 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{6}\\,{e}^{-11\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192523 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbba4fae-4e47-5a69-a6c1-d8754c18767a" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "bd63ff93-762d-5d7d-be8d-9701130b9fe5" :question/math-academy-id "e-11123" :question/problem "Let $X_1, X_2, X_3$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = (\\theta+1) x^{-\\theta-2}, \\qquad x \\geq 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter. Find the likelihood function of the sample $2.0, \\, 4.5, \\,5.1 .$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = (\\theta+1) x^{-\\theta-2}, \\qquad x \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}((\\theta +1){x}_{i}^{-\\theta -2}) \\\\  & =(\\theta +1)^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -2} \\\\  & =(\\theta +1)^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =2.0\\cdot 4.5\\cdot 5.1=45.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = (\\theta+1)^{3} ( 45.9 )^{-\\theta-2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbba4fae-4e47-5a69-a6c1-d8754c18767a" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5548e192-7fc1-5720-a683-fb26a4860eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{33.6}(4)^{-\\theta -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441700} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe4d4c8-7b74-55c2-8864-41564a86d11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{3}(33.6)^{-\\theta -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441711} {:answer/id #uuid "817339c5-e032-5434-8fb9-211e26758437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{33.6}(3)^{-\\theta -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441717} {:answer/id #uuid "9f13f3b0-8682-52a7-a266-73a7860826f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{4}(33.6)^{-\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441730} {:answer/id #uuid "b25835e4-1079-513b-ba55-ed11d841865a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{33.6}(4)^{-\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fe4d4c8-7b74-55c2-8864-41564a86d11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{3}(33.6)^{-\\theta -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441711} :answer-field/id #uuid "16f75826-a1b7-51cc-b472-78495d53facc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3058acb9-d471-587a-a178-41dd1d63905c" :question/math-academy-id "q-159849" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. Find the likelihood function of the sample $2.1,\\,3.2,\\,5.0.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\theta {x}_{i}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -1} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =2.1\\cdot 3.2\\cdot 5.0=33.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\theta }^{3}(33.6)^{-\\theta -1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6068f85b-0ea6-5161-a8d1-d4eb2504852b" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Exponential Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062001ba-598c-5d1c-bc90-3231cae72f17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{-15}\\,{e}^{9\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441687} {:answer/id #uuid "6b2e5acc-40d7-565d-aa81-ff43f9056804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\,{e}^{-15\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441707} {:answer/id #uuid "82aeef2f-9a67-5965-824e-c2396c385705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{9}\\,{e}^{-15\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441719} {:answer/id #uuid "e7bc47a0-30bf-5ee3-bac2-a555bdc2939e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{15}\\,{e}^{-9\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441751} {:answer/id #uuid "fc034778-5c82-5100-98e3-a45ac7a78894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{-9}\\,{e}^{15\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82aeef2f-9a67-5965-824e-c2396c385705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{9}\\,{e}^{-15\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441719} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cc31dfd-996c-5477-8536-ac20e1318f12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1983f891-c0ec-5f2f-97f4-aee1cde66c6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159873" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{9},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=15.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for an exponential random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=9$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=15$ and $n=9.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{9}\\,{e}^{-15\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6068f85b-0ea6-5161-a8d1-d4eb2504852b" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Exponential Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1493f427-3ec7-524c-9d7f-9e81ca26eed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{8}\\,{e}^{\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441688} {:answer/id #uuid "63cf22b5-7f93-5e24-9448-1d7bad4ce711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\,{e}^{-8\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441704} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff0b119-2b3f-582b-99fc-7d672cb6d196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/ma