ndent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\theta {x}_{i}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -1} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=34.2$ and $n=18.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\theta }^{18}(34.2)^{-\\theta -1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbba4fae-4e47-5a69-a6c1-d8754c18767a" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20cfd1ac-3d62-5228-8c42-0e20ad31845d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +1)^{12}(12.1)^{-\\theta -4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441691} {:answer/id #uuid "33c45630-6324-596e-b0b8-8a83b54f9dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +2)^{12.1}(12)^{-\\theta -2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441693} {:answer/id #uuid "6e498b97-1a1d-57c3-b809-f7ea65d1e7a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +4)^{12}(12.1)^{-\\theta -2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441710} {:answer/id #uuid "74738b3f-0d08-536c-91bd-808ed0f7492e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +2)^{12.1}(12)^{-\\theta -3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441714} {:answer/id #uuid "8e6b4048-d8d3-5ef8-bf0c-70f04f6c23d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +2)^{12}(12.1)^{-\\theta -3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e6b4048-d8d3-5ef8-bf0c-70f04f6c23d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +2)^{12}(12.1)^{-\\theta -3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441725} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f9e6a32-13a9-57bb-b2e7-4364b34f9b73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64c12c20-f4ed-568b-9dbd-aefd4fca5ee6" :question/math-academy-id "q-159918" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +2){x}^{-\\theta -3},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{12},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\prod }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}=12.1.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=12$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +2){x}^{-\\theta -3},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}((\\theta +2){x}_{i}^{-\\theta -3}) \\\\  & =(\\theta +2)^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -3} \\\\  & =(\\theta +2)^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=12.1$ and $n=12.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=(\\theta +2)^{12}(12.1)^{-\\theta -3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbba4fae-4e47-5a69-a6c1-d8754c18767a" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d0c091b-9ce4-5fa2-a861-5cac72d393b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +1)(4)^{-\\theta -2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441690} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef9b33e-ad8c-5a33-bee1-fb73b9566c90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +1)(4.5)^{-\\theta -2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441699} {:answer/id #uuid "5adf957f-8b72-5b76-8172-6e139d224976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +1)^{4}(3)^{-\\theta -2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441701} {:answer/id #uuid "76830c10-9f47-5b30-bc5a-29692568d290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +1)^{3}(4.5)^{-\\theta -2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441716} {:answer/id #uuid "dce5567e-0f83-5647-80ce-1c5b710c0f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +1)^{4.5}(4)^{-\\theta -2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76830c10-9f47-5b30-bc5a-29692568d290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +1)^{3}(4.5)^{-\\theta -2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441716} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf45163a-b1a9-5557-a7de-57f1ef3770a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb9c4cb7-5063-5801-b2b1-37e6379336d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-159921" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +1){x}^{-\\theta -2},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. Find the likelihood function of the sample $1.2,\\,1.5,\\,2.5.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +1){x}^{-\\theta -2},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}((\\theta +1){x}_{i}^{-\\theta -2}) \\\\  & =(\\theta +1)^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -2} \\\\  & =(\\theta +1)^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =1.2\\cdot 1.5\\cdot 2.5=4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=(\\theta +1)^{3}(4.5)^{-\\theta -2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbba4fae-4e47-5a69-a6c1-d8754c18767a" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "492a6c1b-401b-53e4-ba39-5675e2455008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +2)^{7.7}(3)^{-\\theta -3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441698} {:answer/id #uuid "8d9403b5-aafb-5d53-aa6d-0e5daf290a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +2)^{3}(7.7)^{-\\theta -3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441723} {:answer/id #uuid "8e460f54-8212-5400-aa16-c056c7b454f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +2)^{3}(6.1)^{-\\theta +3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441724} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6284b9-9a9f-5ee1-920b-8bb95737b84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +2)^{4}(6.1)^{-\\theta -3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441729} {:answer/id #uuid "9f3d6e63-005e-5287-8d47-4bdb79a1a681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +2)^{3}(6.1)^{-\\theta -3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d9403b5-aafb-5d53-aa6d-0e5daf290a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\theta +2)^{3}(7.7)^{-\\theta -3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441723} :answer-field/id #uuid "318e1b68-556e-56da-a5bf-0363656b0f61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6e58c8d-9f78-5f4d-ae53-f347e7b01f68" :question/math-academy-id "q-159923" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +2){x}^{-\\theta -3},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. Find the likelihood function of the sample $1.4,\\,2.2,\\,2.5.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +2){x}^{-\\theta -3},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}((\\theta +2){x}_{i}^{-\\theta -3}) \\\\  & =(\\theta +2)^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -3} \\\\  & =(\\theta +2)^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =1.4\\cdot 2.2\\cdot 2.5=7.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=(\\theta +2)^{3}(7.7)^{-\\theta -3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d363c839-34b6-55c7-85d8-f2013e08cc62" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b755372-db3e-5f16-b999-381ea1404c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.9+5.6\\theta -4{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441689} {:answer/id #uuid "71f464b2-1843-55c2-8084-659dd1b2f728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.8+5.6\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441713} {:answer/id #uuid "761f56de-cba1-57c2-970e-0afc8fffe4e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.9-5.6\\theta +8{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441715} {:answer/id #uuid "877f3031-f27c-5cb3-b54b-bc229959257a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.8+5.6\\theta -4{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441720} {:answer/id #uuid "e42a0815-e856-58aa-a4ab-faf556e096b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.9-5.6\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b755372-db3e-5f16-b999-381ea1404c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.9+5.6\\theta -4{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441689} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d7c05fc-2a50-5093-8512-b4d447e9981f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b9cdfe0-b9cf-519c-9814-c1b778856085" :question/math-academy-id "q-159950" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{8}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{8},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}=5.6,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2}=7.8.\n$$\n\nGiven that the likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{16{\\pi }^{4}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and variance $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=8$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=5.6,$ $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}=7.8,$ and $n=8.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-8/2}\\,{e}^{-(7.8-2(5.6)\\theta +8{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16{\\pi }^{4}}\\,{e}^{\\,-3.9\\,+\\,5.6\\theta \\,-\\,4{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-3.9+5.6\\theta -4{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d363c839-34b6-55c7-85d8-f2013e08cc62" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35e5737f-3a2e-5e55-98b5-0eae615d6728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.12-2.8\\theta -5{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441694} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0cf2ea-a653-5d37-abfc-d145715d04f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/