 is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1.3+0.2+2.7+(-0.7)+0.9 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(1.3)^{2}+(0.2)^{2}+(2.3)^{2}+(-0.7)^{2}+(0.9)^{2} \\\\  & =8.32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-5/2}\\,{e}^{-(8.32-2(4)\\theta +5{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2\\pi )^{5/2}}\\,{e}^{\\,-4.16\\,+\\,4\\theta \\,-\\,2.5{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-4.16+4\\theta -2.5{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #e