.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean.  Given that the likelihood function of the sample $0.8,\\,-0.8,\\,0.6,\\,0.9,\\,2.4$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{(2\\pi )^{5/2}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.8+(-0.8)+0.6+0.9+2.4 \\\\  & =3.9 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.8)^{2}+(-0.8)^{2}+(0.6)^{2}+(0.9)^{2}+(2.4)^{2} \\\\  & =8.21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-5/2}\\,{e}^{-(8.21-2(3.9)\\theta +5{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2\\pi )^{5/2}}\\,{e}^{\\,-4.105\\,+\\,3.9\\theta \\,-\\,2.5{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-4.105+3.9\\theta -2.5{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d363c839-34b6-55c7-85d8-f2013e08cc62" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a145d57-974b-51bc-9dc6-ebf8176d9b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-33.36+8.9\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441706} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7ba093-222f-5f1e-9617-02c352235809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17.8+66.72\\theta -6{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441734} {:answer/id #uuid "aedcc91b-d017-5cb5-9dbc-8b4eaac10489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-66.72+17.8\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441736} {:answer/id #uuid "b628ddf7-b3f4-5124-b7aa-245869f81723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-66.72+8.9\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441738} {:answer/id #uuid "caf15051-75d5-5b6c-b98a-64a022f1e2d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-33.36+17.8\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caf15051-75d5-5b6c-b98a-64a022f1e2d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-33.36+17.8\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441743} :answer-field/id #uuid "273285e8-0feb-51b2-b1b4-ca3ec40e4303" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2015b9da-5691-5067-96fe-7987a1cb7888" :question/math-academy-id "q-159974" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5},{X}_{6}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. Given that the likelihood function of the sample $4.8,\\,0.7,\\,5.0,\\,2.7,\\,1.9,\\,2.7$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =4.8+0.7+5+2.7+1.9+2.7 \\\\  & =17.8 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(4.8)^{2}+(0.7)^{2}+(5)^{2}+(2.7)^{2}+(1.9)^{2}+(2.7)^{2} \\\\  & =66.72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-6/2}\\,{e}^{-(66.72-2(17.8)\\theta +6{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,-33.36\\,+\\,17.8\\theta \\,-\\,3{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-33.36+17.8\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"}]}
